{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96414.pdf|43", "text": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump — 1.2.1.4  Capacity Retention

As outdoor temperatures drop below the rated capacity temperature of 47°F, the available capacity of the ASHP decreases. The capacity retained by an ASHP below this temperature is defined by its capacity retention fraction. This ASHP option's capacity retention fraction is 0.5 at 5°F. This capacity retention factor specifies the fraction of the ASHP's rated capacity available at 5°F. For this ducted ASHP option, its capacity at 47°F-equal to the ACCA Manual J cooling load with ACCA Manual S oversizing allowances added-will be reduced by half at 5°F.", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96414.pdf", "doc_title": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "section": "1.2.1.4  Capacity Retention", "line_start": 209, "line_end": 209, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "5", "measure_name": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 2025 Release 1", "date_last_updated": "2026-05-21", "date_last_updated_source": "pdf_moddate"}}
{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96414.pdf|44", "text": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump — 1.2.1.5  Backup System and Switchover Temperature

This modeled ASHP option uses a natural gas furnace as its backup system. Fossil fuel furnaces and ASHP dual fuel systems cannot provide heating at the same time. Therefore, a switchover temperature must be defined for the ASHP system to switch over to the fossil fuel furnace to fully meet the heating load. To operate this system economically, the switchover temperature is typically set between 25°F and 45°F (Air Source Heat Pump Collaborative 2023). The ideal switchover temperature depends on the efficiencies of the ASHP and furnace systems as well as the prices of electricity and fossil fuel. Lennox recommends a switchover temperature of around 35°F for its products (Lennox 2016). For this measure upgrade, the switchover temperature is also set at 35°F.", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96414.pdf", "doc_title": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "section": "1.2.1.5  Backup System and Switchover Temperature", "line_start": 213, "line_end": 213, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "5", "measure_name": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 2025 Release 1", "date_last_updated": "2026-05-21", "date_last_updated_source": "pdf_moddate"}}
{"id": "upgrade_measures|measure_pdfs/96414.pdf|45", "text": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump — 1.2.1.6  Duct Considerations

With this dual fuel system measure upgrade, there are some circumstances where the ducts in the dwelling are resized when the upgrade option is applied. This occurs if the existing ducts are not large enough to accommodate the ACCA Manual S-sized ASHP being installed. Because ACCA Manual S sized systems are smaller than systems sized for the full heating load of the dwelling, there is less likelihood that duct systems will be resized in this particular measure upgrade.", "metadata": {"product": "resstock", "release": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1", "source_id": "upgrade_measures", "source_type": "pdf", "source_path": "measure_pdfs/96414.pdf", "doc_title": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "section": "1.2.1.6  Duct Considerations", "line_start": 217, "line_end": 217, "corpus_version": "resstock_amy2018_2025_release_1-v1", "status": "osti_pdf", "measure_id": "5", "measure_name": "Dual Fuel Heat Pump", "measure_initial_release": "ResStock 2025 Release 1", "date_last_updated": "2026-05-21", "date_last_updated_source": "pdf_moddate"}}
